[{"input":"# Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity\n\nCalculus was created to describe how quantities change:\n\n- *differentiation*, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and\n- *integration*, for finding a function having a given rate of change.\n\nBoth of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of *limit* of a function.\n\nIn this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called *continuity*.\n","output":"","result":"# Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity\n\nCalculus was created to describe how quantities change:\n\n- *differentiation*, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and\n- *integration*, for finding a function having a given rate of change.\n\nBoth of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of *limit* of a function.\n\nIn this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called *continuity*.\n","resultHTML":"<h1 id=\"md-x8vise-1002-6x8ttp-lecture-3-limits-and-continuity\">Lecture 3: Limits and Continuity</h1>\n<p>Calculus was created to describe how quantities change:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><em>differentiation</em>, for finding the rate of change of a given function, and\n</li>\n<li><em>integration</em>, for finding a function having a given rate of change.\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both of these procedure are based on the fundamental concept of <em>limit</em> of a function.</p>\n<p>In this chapter we will introduce the limit concept and develop some of its properties, including the nice behaviour of some functions that is called <em>continuity</em>.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## Average and Instantaneous Velocity\n\nThe position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object's position by the length of the time interval.\n\n!!! example\n    The average velocity of a falling rock.\n\n    Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first ``t\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` if will fall a distance\n\n    ```math\n    y = \\frac{gt^2}{2}\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\,,\n    ```\n\n    with ``g\\approx 9.8`` a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth's rotation).\n\n    1. What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first ``2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` (time interval ``\\left[0,2\\right]``)?\n    \n       ```math\n       \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-0^2}{2-0}=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n       ```\n\n    2. What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``?\n    \n       ```math\n       \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-1^2}{2-1}=14.7\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n       ```\n\nThe instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant ``t`` can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing ``t``.\n\n!!! example\n    How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``?\n","output":"","result":"## Average and Instantaneous Velocity\n\nThe position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object's position by the length of the time interval.\n\n!!! example\n    The average velocity of a falling rock.\n\n    Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first ``t\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` if will fall a distance\n\n    ```math\n    y = \\frac{gt^2}{2}\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]\\,,\n    ```\n\n    with ``g\\approx 9.8`` a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth's rotation).\n\n    1. What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first ``2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` (time interval ``\\left[0,2\\right]``)?\n    \n       ```math\n       \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-0^2}{2-0}=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n       ```\n\n    2. What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval ``\\left[1,2\\right]``?\n    \n       ```math\n       \\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{t_2^2-t_1^2}{t_2-t_1}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2^2-1^2}{2-1}=14.7\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]\\,.\n       ```\n\nThe instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant ``t`` can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing ``t``.\n\n!!! example\n    How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``?\n","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-x8vise-1004-1wbkm9z-average-and-instantaneous-velocity\">Average and Instantaneous Velocity</h2>\n<p>The position of a moving object is a function of time. The average velocity of the object over a time interval is found by dividing the change in the object’s position by the length of the time interval.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>The average velocity of a falling rock.</p>\n<p>Physical experiments show that if a rock is dropped from rest near the surface of the earth, in the first <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> if will fall a distance</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>g</mi><msup><mi>t</mi><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo></mrow></math>\n<p>with <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≈</mo><mn>9.8</mn></mrow></math> a constant representing the combined action of the gravition (from mass distribution within earth) and centrifugal forces (from the earth’s rotation).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the average velocity of the falling rock during the first <math><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> (time interval <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>0,2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math>)?\n</li>\n</ol>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>9.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<ol>\n<li>What is the average velocity of the falling rock in the time interval <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,2</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math>?\n</li>\n</ol>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msubsup></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>t</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>14.7</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math></div></aside>\n<p>The instantaneous velocity of the object at the instant <math><mi>t</mi></math> can be estimated by evaluating the average velocity in a small time interval containing <math><mi>t</mi></math>.</p>\n<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>?</p></div></aside>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const intervals = [\n  [1, 1.1],\n  [1, 1.01],\n  [1, 1.001],\n  [1, 1.0001],\n];\n\nconst rows = intervals.map(([x1, x2]) => {\n  const fx = 4.9 * (x2 + x1);\n  const roundedFx = String(Number(fx.toFixed(4)));\n\n  return [\n    JSCells.latex(`\\\\left[${x1},${x2}\\\\right]`),\n    JSCells.latex(roundedFx),\n  ];\n});\n\nJSCells.table({\n  headers: [\"Time Interval\", \"Average Velocity\"],\n  rows,\n  align: [\"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"Time IntervalAverage Velocity[1,1.1]10.29[1,1.01]9.849[1,1.001]9.8049[1,1.0001]9.8005","resultHTML":"<table class=\"jscells-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"jscells-align-center\">Time Interval</th><th class=\"jscells-align-center\">Average Velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>10.29</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.01</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.849</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.8049</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>1,1.0001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>9.8005</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"!!! example\n    How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``?","output":"","result":"!!! example\n    How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``?","resultHTML":"<aside class=\"markdown-admonition markdown-admonition-example\"><div class=\"markdown-admonition-title\">Example</div><div class=\"markdown-admonition-body\"><p>How fast is the rock of the previous example falling at time <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>?</p></div></aside>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const intervals = [\n  [2, 2.1],\n  [2, 2.01],\n  [2, 2.001],\n  [2, 2.0001],\n];\n\nconst rows = intervals.map(([x1, x2]) => {\n  const fx = 4.9 * (x2 + x1);\n  const roundedFx = String(Number(fx.toFixed(4)));\n\n  return [\n    JSCells.latex(`\\\\left[${x1},${x2}\\\\right]`),\n    JSCells.latex(roundedFx),\n  ];\n});\n\nJSCells.table({\n  headers: [\"Time Interval\", \"Average Velocity\"],\n  rows,\n  align: [\"center\", \"center\"],\n});","output":"","result":"Time IntervalAverage Velocity[2,2.1]20.09[2,2.01]19.649[2,2.001]19.6049[2,2.0001]19.6005","resultHTML":"<table class=\"jscells-table\"><thead><tr><th class=\"jscells-align-center\">Time Interval</th><th class=\"jscells-align-center\">Average Velocity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>20.09</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.01</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.649</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.6049</mn></math></span></td></tr><tr><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mn>2,2.0001</mn><mo fence=\"true\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math></span></td><td class=\"jscells-align-center\"><span class=\"jscells-latex\"><math><mn>19.6005</mn></math></span></td></tr></tbody></table>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval ``\\left[t,t+h\\right]`` is\n\n```math\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{\\left(t+h\\right)^2-t^2}{\\left(t+h\\right)-t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\n```\n\nWe examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths ``h`` became smaller and smaller. We were finding the *limit of the average velocity as ``h`` tends to zero*. This is expressed symbolically in the form\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\\,.\n```\n\nWe can't find the limit of the fraction by just substituting ``h=0`` because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\left(2t+h\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe final form no longer involves division by ``h``. It approaches ``gt + \\frac{g}{2}0=gt``. In particular, at ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` and ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``, the instantaneous velocities are ``v_1=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]`` and ``v_2=19.6\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]``, respectively.","output":"","result":"In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval ``\\left[t,t+h\\right]`` is\n\n```math\n\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{\\left(t+h\\right)^2-t^2}{\\left(t+h\\right)-t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\n```\n\nWe examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths ``h`` became smaller and smaller. We were finding the *limit of the average velocity as ``h`` tends to zero*. This is expressed symbolically in the form\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\frac{2th+h^2}{h}\\,.\n```\n\nWe can't find the limit of the fraction by just substituting ``h=0`` because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:\n\n```math\n\\lim_{h\\to0}\\frac{\\Delta y}{\\Delta t}=\\frac{g}{2}\\left(2t+h\\right)\\,.\n```\n\nThe final form no longer involves division by ``h``. It approaches ``gt + \\frac{g}{2}0=gt``. In particular, at ``t=1\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]`` and ``t=2\\,\\left[\\mathrm{s}\\right]``, the instantaneous velocities are ``v_1=9.8\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]`` and ``v_2=19.6\\,\\left[\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{s}}\\right]``, respectively.","resultHTML":"<p>In the second example the average velocity of the falling rock over the time interval <math><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mi>t</mi><mo separator=\"true\">,</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></math> is</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><msup><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn class=\"tml-med-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<p>We examined the values of this average velocity for time intervals whose lengths <math><mi>h</mi></math> became smaller and smaller. We were finding the <em>limit of the average velocity as <math><mi>h</mi></math> tends to zero</em>. This is expressed symbolically in the form</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>h</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>We can’t find the limit of the fraction by just substituting <math><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> because that would involve dividing by zero. However, we can calculate the limit by first performing som algebraic simplifications on the expression:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><munder><mi>lim</mi><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Δ</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>The final form no longer involves division by <math><mi>h</mi></math>. It approaches <math><mrow><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>g</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mi>g</mi><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. In particular, at <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, the instantaneous velocities are <math><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>9.8</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math> and <math><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>19.6</mn><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">[</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mfrac><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">]</mo></mrow></mrow></math>, respectively.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"## The Area of a Circle\n\nAll circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference ``C`` to the diameter ``2r`` has the same value for all circles. The number ``\\uppi `` is defined to be this common ratio:\n\n```math\n\\frac{C}{2r}=\\uppi \\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad C=2\\uppi r\\,.\n```\n\nWe were taught that the area ``A`` of a circle is this same number ``\\uppi `` times the square of the radius:\n\n```math\nA=\\uppi r^2\\,.\n```\n\nCan we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?\n\nThe answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a \"limit\" of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.\n\nSuppose a regular polygon having ``n`` sides is inscribed in a circle of radius ``r``.","output":"","result":"## The Area of a Circle\n\nAll circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference ``C`` to the diameter ``2r`` has the same value for all circles. The number ``\\uppi `` is defined to be this common ratio:\n\n```math\n\\frac{C}{2r}=\\uppi \\quad\\textrm{or}\\quad C=2\\uppi r\\,.\n```\n\nWe were taught that the area ``A`` of a circle is this same number ``\\uppi `` times the square of the radius:\n\n```math\nA=\\uppi r^2\\,.\n```\n\nCan we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?\n\nThe answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a \"limit\" of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.\n\nSuppose a regular polygon having ``n`` sides is inscribed in a circle of radius ``r``.","resultHTML":"<h2 id=\"md-x8vise-1009-zskrj0-the-area-of-a-circle\">The Area of a Circle</h2>\n<p>All circles are similar geometric figures; they all have the same shape and differ only in size. The ratio of the circumference <math><mi>C</mi></math> to the diameter <math><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> has the same value for all circles. The number <math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></math> is defined to be this common ratio:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mfrac><mi>C</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mtext>or</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\"></mspace><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>r</mi><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>We were taught that the area <math><mi>A</mi></math> of a circle is this same number <math><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></math> times the square of the radius:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>.</mi></mrow></math>\n<p>Can we deduce this area formula from the formula for the circumference?</p>\n<p>The answer to this question lies in regarding the circle as a “limit” of regular polygons, which are in turn made up of triangles.</p>\n<p>Suppose a regular polygon having <math><mi>n</mi></math> sides is inscribed in a circle of radius <math><mi>r</mi></math>.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null},{"input":"const width = 320;\nconst height = 302;\nconst r = 150;\nconst xmid = 151;\nconst ymid = 151;\n\n// Approximate label dimensions for the LaTeX labels.\nconst fontX = 16;\nconst fontY = 24;\n\nconst figure = JSCells.figure(\n  { width, height },\n  \"A regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.\"\n);\n\nfigure.circle()\n  .cx(xmid).cy(ymid).r(r)\n  .fill(\"none\").stroke(\"#ff0000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nconst points = Array.from({ length: 10 }, (_, i) => {\n  const t = (2 * Math.PI * i) / 9;\n  return [xmid + r * Math.cos(t), ymid + r * Math.sin(t)];\n});\n\nfor (let i = 0; i < 9; i += 1) {\n  figure.line()\n    .x1(points[i][0]).y1(points[i][1])\n    .x2(points[i + 1][0]).y2(points[i + 1][1])\n    .stroke(\"#008000\").strokeWidth(1);\n}\n\nconst p1 = points[0];\nconst p9 = points[8];\nconst midpoint = [(p1[0] + p9[0]) / 2, (p1[1] + p9[1]) / 2];\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(p1[0]).y2(p1[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(p9[0]).y2(p9[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1);\n\nfigure.line()\n  .x1(xmid).y1(xmid).x2(midpoint[0]).y2(midpoint[1])\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").strokeWidth(1)\n  .setAttribute(\"stroke-dasharray\", \"3\");\n\nfigure.label(\"O\", {\n  x: xmid - fontX,\n  y: ymid - 0.5 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"r\", {\n  x: xmid + 0.5 * r - 0.5 * fontX,\n  y: ymid,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"A\", {\n  x: 305,\n  y: ymid - 0.5 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"B\", {\n  x: p9[0] + 0.25 * fontX,\n  y: p9[1] - 0.75 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\nfigure.label(\"M\", {\n  x: midpoint[0] - 1.5 * fontX,\n  y: midpoint[1] - 0.75 * fontY,\n  width: fontX,\n  height: fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nconst arcEnd = [\n  75 * Math.cos(-Math.PI / 9) + xmid + 1.5,\n  75 * Math.sin(-Math.PI / 9) + ymid + 1.75,\n];\n\nfigure.path()\n  .d(`M 226 151 A 75 75 0 0 0 ${arcEnd[0]} ${arcEnd[1]}`)\n  .stroke(\"#000000\").fill(\"none\");\n\n// Draw the arrowhead as a polygon because the SVG helper doesn't expose markers.\nconst direction = [-Math.sin(Math.PI / 9), -Math.cos(Math.PI / 9)];\nconst perpendicular = [-direction[1], direction[0]];\nconst arrowLength = 6;\nconst arrowHalfWidth = 3;\nconst arrowBase = [\n  arcEnd[0] - arrowLength * direction[0],\n  arcEnd[1] - arrowLength * direction[1],\n];\n\nfigure.polygon().points([\n  arcEnd,\n  [\n    arrowBase[0] + arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[0],\n    arrowBase[1] + arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[1],\n  ],\n  [\n    arrowBase[0] - arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[0],\n    arrowBase[1] - arrowHalfWidth * perpendicular[1],\n  ],\n].map(([x, y]) => `${x},${y}`).join(\" \"))\n  .fill(\"#000000\");\n\nfigure.label(String.raw`\\frac{\\pi}{n}`, {\n  x: xmid + 80 * Math.cos(-Math.PI / 18),\n  y: ymid + 80 * Math.sin(-Math.PI / 9),\n  width: fontX,\n  height: 2 * fontY,\n  latex: true,\n});\n\nfigure;","output":"","result":"OrABMπnA regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.","resultHTML":"<figure><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"320\" height=\"302\" viewBox=\"0 0 320 302\"><circle cx=\"151\" cy=\"151\" r=\"150\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#ff0000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></circle><line x1=\"301\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"265.9066664678467\" y2=\"247.41814145298088\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"265.9066664678467\" y1=\"247.41814145298088\" x2=\"177.04722665003956\" y2=\"298.72116295183116\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"177.04722665003956\" y1=\"298.72116295183116\" x2=\"76.00000000000003\" y2=\"280.9038105676658\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"76.00000000000003\" y1=\"280.9038105676658\" x2=\"10.046106882113747\" y2=\"202.30302149885034\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"10.046106882113747\" y1=\"202.30302149885034\" x2=\"10.046106882113747\" y2=\"99.6969785011497\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"10.046106882113747\" y1=\"99.6969785011497\" x2=\"75.99999999999993\" y2=\"21.096189432334256\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"75.99999999999993\" y1=\"21.096189432334256\" x2=\"177.0472266500395\" y2=\"3.278837048168782\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"177.0472266500395\" y1=\"3.278837048168782\" x2=\"265.90666646784666\" y2=\"54.58185854701907\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"265.90666646784666\" y1=\"54.58185854701907\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"150.99999999999997\" stroke=\"#008000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"301\" y2=\"151\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"265.90666646784666\" y2=\"54.58185854701907\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\"></line><line x1=\"151\" y1=\"151\" x2=\"283.45333323392333\" y2=\"102.79092927350953\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"3\"></line><foreignObject x=\"135\" y=\"139\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>O</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"218\" y=\"151\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>r</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"305\" y=\"139\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>A</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"269.90666646784666\" y=\"36.58185854701907\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>B</mi></math></div></foreignObject><foreignObject x=\"259.45333323392333\" y=\"84.79092927350953\" width=\"16\" height=\"24\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mi>M</mi></math></div></foreignObject><path d=\"M 226 151 A 75 75 0 0 0 222.97694655894313 127.09848925057484\" stroke=\"#000000\" fill=\"none\"></path><polygon points=\"222.97694655894313,127.09848925057484 227.84814528125486,131.71058454531328 222.20998955653943,133.7627054052673\" fill=\"#000000\"></polygon><foreignObject x=\"229.78462024097664\" y=\"123.63838853394651\" width=\"16\" height=\"48\"><div xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"><math><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mfrac></math></div></foreignObject></svg><figcaption>A regular polygon (green) of n sides inscribed in a red circle. Here n = 9.</figcaption></figure>","hidden":true,"type":"code"},{"input":"The perimeter ``P_n`` and the area ``A_n`` of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference ``C`` and the area ``A`` of the circle, but if ``n`` is large ``P_n`` is close to ``C`` and ``A_n`` is close to ``A``. We could expect ``P_n`` to approach the limit ``C`` and ``A_n`` to approach the limit ``A`` as ``n`` tends to infinity.\n\nSince the total angle around the point ``O`` is ``2\\uppi `` radians, ``\\angle AOB`` is ``\\frac{2\\uppi }{n}`` radians. If ``M`` is the midpoint of ``AB``, then ``O`` bisects ``\\angle AOB``. We can write the length of ``AB`` and the area of ``\\triangle OAB`` in terms of the radius:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|AB\\right|=&2\\left|AM\\right|=2r\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\n\\triangle OAB=&\\frac{1}{2}\\left|AB\\right|\\left|OM\\right|=r^2\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nThe perimeter ``P_n`` and area ``A_n`` of the polygon are ``n`` times these expressions:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n=&2rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\nA_n=&r^2n\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nSolving the first equation for ``rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{P_n}{2}`` and substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n```math\nA_n = \\frac{1}{2}P_nr\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n```\n\nNow ``\\angle AOM=\\frac{\\uppi}{n}`` approaches ``0`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, so its cosine, ``\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{\\left|OM\\right|}{\\left|OA\\right|}``, approaches ``1``. Since ``P_n`` approaches ``C=2\\uppi r`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, the expression for ``A_n`` approaches ``\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2\\uppi r\\right)r\\left(1\\right)=\\uppi r^2``, which must therefore be the area of the circle.\n\n","output":"","result":"The perimeter ``P_n`` and the area ``A_n`` of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference ``C`` and the area ``A`` of the circle, but if ``n`` is large ``P_n`` is close to ``C`` and ``A_n`` is close to ``A``. We could expect ``P_n`` to approach the limit ``C`` and ``A_n`` to approach the limit ``A`` as ``n`` tends to infinity.\n\nSince the total angle around the point ``O`` is ``2\\uppi `` radians, ``\\angle AOB`` is ``\\frac{2\\uppi }{n}`` radians. If ``M`` is the midpoint of ``AB``, then ``O`` bisects ``\\angle AOB``. We can write the length of ``AB`` and the area of ``\\triangle OAB`` in terms of the radius:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\left|AB\\right|=&2\\left|AM\\right|=2r\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\n\\triangle OAB=&\\frac{1}{2}\\left|AB\\right|\\left|OM\\right|=r^2\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nThe perimeter ``P_n`` and area ``A_n`` of the polygon are ``n`` times these expressions:\n\n```math\n\\begin{aligned}\nP_n=&2rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\\\\nA_n=&r^2n\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n\\end{aligned}\n```\n\nSolving the first equation for ``rn\\sin\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{P_n}{2}`` and substituting into the second equation, we get\n\n```math\nA_n = \\frac{1}{2}P_nr\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}\n```\n\nNow ``\\angle AOM=\\frac{\\uppi}{n}`` approaches ``0`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, so its cosine, ``\\cos\\frac{\\uppi}{n}=\\frac{\\left|OM\\right|}{\\left|OA\\right|}``, approaches ``1``. Since ``P_n`` approaches ``C=2\\uppi r`` as ``n`` tends to infinity, the expression for ``A_n`` approaches ``\\frac{1}{2}\\left(2\\uppi r\\right)r\\left(1\\right)=\\uppi r^2``, which must therefore be the area of the circle.\n\n","resultHTML":"<p>The perimeter <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> and the area <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> of the polygon are, respectively, less than the circumference <math><mi>C</mi></math> and the area <math><mi>A</mi></math> of the circle, but if <math><mi>n</mi></math> is large <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> is close to <math><mi>C</mi></math> and <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> is close to <math><mi>A</mi></math>. We could expect <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> to approach the limit <math><mi>C</mi></math> and <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> to approach the limit <math><mi>A</mi></math> as <math><mi>n</mi></math> tends to infinity.</p>\n<p>Since the total angle around the point <math><mi>O</mi></math> is <math><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow></math> radians, <math><mrow><mi>∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is <math><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></math> radians. If <math><mi>M</mi></math> is the midpoint of <math><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, then <math><mi>O</mi></math> bisects <math><mrow><mi>∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. We can write the length of <math><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and the area of <math><mrow><mi>△</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> in terms of the radius:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" class=\"tml-jot\"><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mi>△</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>\n<p>The perimeter <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> and area <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> of the polygon are <math><mi>n</mi></math> times these expressions:</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mtable displaystyle=\"true\" class=\"tml-jot\"><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class=\"tml-right\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class=\"tml-left\" style=\"padding-left:0em;padding-right:0em;\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></math>\n<p>Solving the first equation for <math><mrow><mi>r</mi><mi>n</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mrow></math> and substituting into the second equation, we get</p>\n<math display=\"block\" class=\"tml-display\" style=\"display:block math;\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></math>\n<p>Now <math><mrow><mi>∠</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>O</mi><mi>M</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mrow></math> approaches <math><mn>0</mn></math> as <math><mi>n</mi></math> tends to infinity, so its cosine, <math><mrow><mrow><mi>cos</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mspace width=\"0.1667em\"></mspace></mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>n</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>M</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo><mi>O</mi><mi>A</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">|</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math>, approaches <math><mn>1</mn></math>. Since <math><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> approaches <math><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> as <math><mi>n</mi></math> tends to infinity, the expression for <math><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></math> approaches <math><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><mi>r</mi><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\" stretchy=\"true\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\" stretchy=\"true\">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">π</mi><mspace></mspace></mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mn class=\"tml-sml-pad\">2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, which must therefore be the area of the circle.</p>","hidden":true,"type":"markdown","translations":{},"translationSource":null}]